-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### A1. Benefits
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC While not intended as a permanent fixture, HMS federation offers important benefits, particularly in a migration scenario:
-- MAGIC - **Seamless onboarding:** Data in Hive can be crawled and made available instantly in Unity Catalog, with schemas and table names mirrored.
-- MAGIC - **Fine-grained governance:** You can apply Unity Catalog’s privileges, row filters, column masking, and auditing on federated tables—which is not possible in legacy Hive alone.
-- MAGIC - **Cross-platform continuity:** Legacy workflows, downstream integrations, and code can continue functioning while gradually transitioning to UC features. 
-- MAGIC - **Minimized code change:** Federation reduces the burden of urgent rewrites and coordination between dependent workloads during migration.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### A2. Downsides
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC While HMS Federation dramatically eases migration, there are important things to keep in mind about its long-term use:
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC - **Read vs. write limitations:** External metastores and Glue support read-only access; only internal metastores can be federated for writes. Organizations hoping for deep integration or ongoing ETL will be limited. 
-- MAGIC - **Missing UC features:** HMS Federation does not support everything; for example streaming tables, some materialized view operations, and Apache Spark Declarative Pipelines. Certain UC advanced features will only work with fully migrated Unity Catalog-managed tables. 
-- MAGIC - **Complexity and risk:** Long-term hybrid catalogs can mean added operational complexity, especially in access management, table path overlap, and metadata sync. 
-- MAGIC - **Migration is still needed:** The recommended path is to eventually migrate all Hive tables to Unity Catalog, fully leveraging UC’s capabilities and performance optimizations

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC ### A3. Setting Up HMS Federation
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC <div style="border-left: 4px solid #1976d2; background: #e3f2fd; padding: 16px 20px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 16px 0;">
-- MAGIC   <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px;">
-- MAGIC     <span style="font-size: 24px;">ℹ️</span>
-- MAGIC     <div>
-- MAGIC       <ul>
-- MAGIC         <li>Setting up HMS federation requires metastore admin privileges, or the <code>CREATE CONNECTION</code> privilege on the metastore.</li>
-- MAGIC         <li>Creating the catalog requires you to setup up an external location covering the storage location of your workspace's HMS. UCX can help you identify this location.</li>
-- MAGIC       <ul>
-- MAGIC     </div>
-- MAGIC   </div>
-- MAGIC </div>
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Creating storage credentials is done through the **Catalog Explorer** user interface (this functionality is also available through the API).
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. From **Catalog Explorer**, select **Create &gt; Create a connection**.
-- MAGIC 1. Fill in a unique **Connection name**.
-- MAGIC 1. Select *Hive Metastore* for **Connection type**.
-- MAGIC 1. For **Metastore type**, select *Internal*.
-- MAGIC 1. Fill in the **IAM role (ARN)** field.
-- MAGIC 1. Click **Next**.
-- MAGIC 1. Leave directory set to */user/hive/warehouse* (unless HMS was originally configured differently, which is unlikely) and click **Create connection**.
-- MAGIC 1. Adjust the **Catalog name** if desired, then click **Create catalog**.
-- MAGIC

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ## B. Migrating Data Objects

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### B1. Tables
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC There are several distinct scenarios for migrating tables from HMS to UC, depending on table type and storage location. Primarily, these depend on where the table data is being stored. Each scenario requires a different migration path and methodology.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC From a technical point of view, it's important to note that none of these upgrade paths are inherently difficult. But they do vary in impact with how well they can work during a transitional period, and it's important to understand these implications in planning a widescale migration.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC #### B1.1 Managed Tables with Data on DBFS Root
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC This represents the "accept all defaults" scenario; a managed HMS table whose data is stored in the default storage location (typically DBFS root).
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Due to security restrictions, we can't register a location within DBFS root in the UC metastore, so we cannot register it as an external table in UC. The only option is to copy the table data from its location in DBFS root over to UC managed storage.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC There are two options to do this:
-- MAGIC - `CREATE TABLE AS SELECT`: a universal approach that creates a fresh copy of the table from any supported source
-- MAGIC - `DEEP CLONE`: retains table metadata (can only be used for Delta source tables)
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC But, as mentioned, both options create a new copy of the table (data and all) in the UC metastore, and here are the unavoidable downsides to this approach:
-- MAGIC - **Data movement:** since data is being physically copied, there is a time (and potential cost) associated with that. This might be  negligible for small datasets, but can become a challenge for larger ones.
-- MAGIC - **Duplicated data:** the bigger concern is that we now have two copies of the data. Legacy workloads will continue to operate on the old table, so the migration plan has to include a way to manage this and keep changes in sync until a complete cutover.
-- MAGIC
